```java
// MySQL — note the params after the ? configure SSL + timezone
String mysql = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/shop?useSSL=false&serverTimezone=UTC";
// PostgreSQL — different default port (5432) and its own params
String pg    = "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/shop?sslmode=disable";
// Oracle (thin driver) — uses a SID/service form, not the //host pattern
String ora   = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@localhost:1521:XE";

// DriverManager reads the subprotocol ("mysql") to choose the registered driver
try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(mysql, "user", "pass")) {
    System.out.println("connected to " + con.getCatalog()); // prints: shop
}
```

*The subprotocol (`mysql`, `postgresql`, `oracle:thin`) is the only part that selects the driver — everything else just locates the database.*

```java
Connection con = null;
PreparedStatement ps = null;
try {
    con = dataSource.getConnection();
    ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT 1");
    ps.executeQuery();
} finally {
    // verbose, and forgetting one close() leaks a connection
    if (ps != null) ps.close();
    if (con != null) con.close();   // miss this on an early return -> leak
}
```

```java
try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection();
     PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT 1");
     ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
    while (rs.next()) { /* read rows */ }
}  // rs, then ps, then con auto-closed in reverse order — even if an exception is thrown
```

*Declared in the `try` header, closed automatically in reverse order of declaration. No leaks, no boilerplate.*

```java
// Anti-pattern: every call pays the TCP handshake + auth cost again
for (int id : ids) {
    try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass)) {
        // brand-new physical connection just to run one SELECT
        runQuery(con, id);
    } // connection destroyed; next loop opens another from scratch
}
```

```java
// dataSource is a HikariCP pool created once and shared
for (int id : ids) {
    try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection()) {
        // borrowed from the pool, no handshake
        runQuery(con, id);
    } // close() returns it to the pool for the next iteration
}
```

*Same loop, but the pooled version reuses a handful of warm connections instead of recreating one per query.*

```java
HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();
cfg.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/shop");
cfg.setUsername("app");
cfg.setPassword(System.getenv("DB_PASS"));   // never hard-code
cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(10);          // bounded connections
cfg.setConnectionTimeout(30000);     // wait up to 30s for a free one
DataSource ds = new HikariDataSource(cfg);

try (Connection con = ds.getConnection()) {  // borrow from pool
    // ... use it ...
}                                             // returned to pool, not destroyed
```

*In Spring Boot you just set `spring.datasource.*` and get a HikariCP pool automatically.*

```java
// 1) driver JAR on the classpath (auto-registered) — no Class.forName needed
String url  = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/shop?serverTimezone=UTC"; // 2 the URL
String user = System.getenv("DB_USER");   // 3 from env/secret, not hard-coded
String pass = System.getenv("DB_PASS");   // 4 from env/secret

try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass)) {
    System.out.println("connected: " + con.getMetaData().getDatabaseProductName());
}
```

*A `DataSource` bundles these four settings and adds pooling, so production code asks the DataSource for connections.*

```java
try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass)) {
    // brand-new physical connection (TCP + auth handshake)
}  // close() actually destroys the connection
```

```java
try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection()) {
    // borrowed from the pool — no handshake
}  // close() returns it to the pool for the next caller
```

*Identical-looking code, but the pooled version reuses connections instead of recreating them.*

```java
// You write standard JDBC; the driver does the vendor-specific work.
try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
        "jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/shop", "u", "p");
     PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = ?")) {
    ps.setInt(1, 1);
    try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
        // the Postgres driver turned your call into the PG wire protocol,
        // and turned the response rows back into this ResultSet
        if (rs.next()) System.out.println(rs.getString("name"));
    }
}
```
